Application Control names the application behind each connection with
the open nDPI library (OPNsense's package ndpi), and keeps the servers
it names in pf tables: one for every category nDPI knows and one for
each application chosen, each an External alias in Firewall: Aliases
for rules to use, with an expiry. It reads a copy of the traffic of the
internal interfaces (tcpdump -p, no port made promiscuous) and stands
in no connection's way. A connection named only by its port or address
is shown as a guess and put in no table; those nDPI does not name are
listed with the server name and the JA4 fingerprint their TLS client
sent. On by default.

An application chosen to be blocked - none by default - gets a block rule
of the plugin's on its table, which stops a new connection to its servers,
and each connection nDPI names as it is cut: its pf states killed by its
two ends and, where os-xgs-npu is installed, dropped on the coprocessor
first. Its table takes a server only when the firewall's own resolver
gives that address for the name the connection carried. What was dropped
there is handed back once the application is no longer blocked.

The Rules tab acts on an application for the devices of a source - an
interface's network, a group's, an alias, an address or a network, or
every device but one of them. Block puts the plugin's block rule on the
application's table from that source alone, cuts its connections from
there, and writes a Suricata rule for OPNsense's own Intrusion Detection;
Alert writes the Suricata rule alone. A Suricata rule matches the names
the installed nDPI knows the application by - TLS and QUIC server names,
HTTP's Host, DNS questions - as nDPI matches them, or the protocol when
Suricata reads it, on a device's way to the internet alone, never to the
firewall or its own networks. Suricata tests each new rules file first,
and the lines follow their sources as they change. The tab says what each
rule does against what the last Apply put in force.

